WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.620 --> 00:00:05.320
Six years after the original film, Frozen
II has finally arrived in theaters.

00:00:05.680 --> 00:00:08.220
But do the new songs work as well as the old
ones?

00:00:08.460 --> 00:00:10.660
Are the new characters worth falling in love
with?

00:00:10.860 --> 00:00:13.000
Does Olaf still love warm hugs?

00:00:13.340 --> 00:00:17.880
Here's a spoiler-filled look at the five best
and five worst things in Frozen II.

00:00:19.120 --> 00:00:24.080
Frozen launched into the stratosphere as one
of the biggest family films ever in 2013 for

00:00:24.090 --> 00:00:28.660
a number of reasons, but one of the most important
was its centerpiece song, "Let It Go," which

00:00:28.660 --> 00:00:31.770
any parent can no doubt recite from bitter
memory.

00:00:31.770 --> 00:00:36.220
But while there are certainly attempts in
Frozen 2 to recapture some of that "Let It

00:00:36.220 --> 00:00:40.820
Go" magic, even the planned showstopper "Into
the Unknown" doesn't quite reach those heights.

00:00:40.940 --> 00:00:44.620
It was a tall order, to be sure, but the film
can't quite rise to it.

00:00:45.620 --> 00:00:50.300
Still, if you don't compare the songs to "Let
it Go," there are joys to be found, especially

00:00:50.309 --> 00:00:55.089
in Kristoff's goofy power ballad "Lost in
the Woods," which gives Broadway star-turned-Disney

00:00:55.089 --> 00:00:59.220
darling Jonathan Groff a chance to do his
best REO Speedwagon impression.

00:00:59.420 --> 00:01:03.540
Add in a chorus of reindeer backup singers,
and it's the most pleasant surprise in the

00:01:03.540 --> 00:01:04.040
film.

00:01:04.940 --> 00:01:09.680
The Frozen films might be for kids of all
ages, but they're also inventive fantasy adventures

00:01:09.680 --> 00:01:13.560
that employ their own rich sense of mythology,
magic, and worldbuilding.

00:01:13.720 --> 00:01:18.620
It's a lot of backstory for kids to understand,
so how does Frozen 2 address this?

00:01:18.900 --> 00:01:23.520
Have Olaf stop everything to give the new
characters what's essentially a recap of the

00:01:23.520 --> 00:01:24.140
first film.

00:01:24.280 --> 00:01:28.760
There's nothing here that Michael Peña didn't
do much better in the Ant-Man films over in

00:01:28.760 --> 00:01:30.360
the Marvel Cinematic Universe.

00:01:30.620 --> 00:01:35.220
Unlike those movies, though, in Frozen 2 it
just stops the story dead in its tracks for

00:01:35.220 --> 00:01:37.560
a exposition-heavy nostalgia slog.

00:01:37.700 --> 00:01:39.760
There had to be a better way to do this.

00:01:40.800 --> 00:01:43.320
The first Frozen featured some beautiful animation.

00:01:43.740 --> 00:01:45.560
So how could Frozen 2 hope to top it?

00:01:45.920 --> 00:01:50.060
Directors Chris Buck and Jennifer Lee made
the somewhat gutsy decision to get the lead

00:01:50.060 --> 00:01:54.701
characters out of the familiar confines of
Arendelle and winter, bringing them to a magic

00:01:54.701 --> 00:01:57.100
forest stuck in perpetual autumn.

00:01:57.200 --> 00:01:59.400
That unlocks a new visual creativeness.

00:01:59.900 --> 00:02:04.630
From Elsa's battle against the ocean itself
to her fight against the fire salamander,

00:02:04.630 --> 00:02:07.280
Frozen 2 is absolutely stunning to look at
throughout.

00:02:08.240 --> 00:02:12.560
It's been six years since Frozen hit theaters,
but as the characters helpfully tell us in

00:02:12.569 --> 00:02:16.750
the sequel, only three years have passed in
the realm of Arendelle since the last time

00:02:16.750 --> 00:02:19.220
we saw Anna, Elsa, Olaf, and Kristoff.

00:02:19.300 --> 00:02:23.120
And absolutely nothing whatsoever seems to
have happened in that time.

00:02:23.300 --> 00:02:27.540
Everyone's basically in the exact same place
they were when the last film left them — and

00:02:27.549 --> 00:02:32.109
considering how complex much of Frozen's storytelling
is, that feels like a misstep.

00:02:33.219 --> 00:02:37.450
The static nature of the characters at the
start of the movie feels particularly jarring

00:02:37.450 --> 00:02:41.810
considering Frozen 2 is actually about growing
up and becoming different people.

00:02:41.810 --> 00:02:47.480
Elsa, Anna, Kristoff, and even Olaf all wrestle
with the reality of growing up — and possibly

00:02:47.480 --> 00:02:48.560
growing apart.

00:02:49.000 --> 00:02:52.160
That's just one of the mature themes Frozen
2 touches on.

00:02:52.340 --> 00:02:56.579
The sequel explores the nature of legacy,
the sins of the past, and the ways in which

00:02:56.580 --> 00:03:00.400
we can redeem each other through deliberate
attempts to heal old divisions.

00:03:00.620 --> 00:03:04.860
The plot doesn't always work, and younger
viewers might not understand the themes, but

00:03:04.860 --> 00:03:06.700
Frozen 2 doesn't lack ambition.

00:03:06.920 --> 00:03:10.300
It's trying to say something, and that's…
saying something.

00:03:11.240 --> 00:03:16.450
Disney has a long history of introducing memorable
new characters in sequels to keep things fresh.

00:03:16.450 --> 00:03:21.060
Toy Story, for instance, has Jessie, Bullseye,
and Forky just to name a few.

00:03:21.100 --> 00:03:26.019
But while Frozen 2 introduces some new characters,
none of them seem particularly interesting,

00:03:26.020 --> 00:03:27.960
or important to the story.

00:03:28.040 --> 00:03:33.060
Even the film itself doesn't seem very interested
in the characters, introducing them only to

00:03:33.060 --> 00:03:35.160
pretty much write them out in the next scene.

00:03:35.400 --> 00:03:39.370
If you can remember the names of any of the
Arendelle soldiers or the Northuldra

00:03:39.370 --> 00:03:42.280
people by the end of the film, it will be
a miracle.

00:03:43.020 --> 00:03:47.560
A big reason Frozen worked, particularly with
adults, was the chemistry of the cast.

00:03:47.660 --> 00:03:52.660
And thankfully, six years of waiting did nothing
to diminish the voice cast's tremendous rapport

00:03:52.660 --> 00:03:54.040
with one another on-screen.

00:03:54.120 --> 00:03:58.420
Kristen Bell, Idina Menzel,
Josh Gad, and Jonathan Groff can all still

00:03:58.420 --> 00:04:02.820
fire jokes off each other with incredible
precision, whether they're diving into Anna

00:04:02.820 --> 00:04:05.720
and Kristoff's awkwardness or Olaf's curiosity.

00:04:05.880 --> 00:04:10.680
Even beyond that, though, there's a certain
emotional honesty at play in their performances,

00:04:10.680 --> 00:04:14.240
particularly between Bell and Menzel, that
makes everything land.

00:04:14.240 --> 00:04:18.580
It's nice to see that the magic of the cast
didn't fade at all in the intervening years.

00:04:19.460 --> 00:04:23.200
Olaf's recaps aren't the only bit of clumsy
exposition in the movie.

00:04:23.460 --> 00:04:27.820
Frozen 2 seems so intent on getting started
that they kinda botch the setup.

00:04:28.040 --> 00:04:31.840
After a short flashback with Anna 
and Elsa's parents telling a bedtime story

00:04:31.840 --> 00:04:37.030
that serves as a massive plot dump, we segue right into Elsa hearing voices,

00:04:37.030 --> 00:04:41.180
magically activating all the elements, and
nearly blowing up the city in a rushed sequence

00:04:41.180 --> 00:04:42.960
that feels fairly incoherent.

00:04:43.730 --> 00:04:48.120
By the end of the film, the beginning finally
makes sense — which is great for an experimental

00:04:48.120 --> 00:04:51.250
film like Memento, but not so great for a
kid's movie.

00:04:52.300 --> 00:04:57.030
At one point in the film, Olaf rather off-handedly
remarks that "water has memory."

00:04:57.030 --> 00:05:01.840
That turns out to be an important line, though,
as it sets up a key part of the Frozen 2 mythos.

00:05:02.240 --> 00:05:06.560
It turns out that water does have memory,
which means ice can hold memory, allowing

00:05:06.560 --> 00:05:09.639
Elsa to become a kind of memory summoner and
keeper.

00:05:09.639 --> 00:05:14.340
This all culminates in one of the film's most
visually gorgeous sequences in the ice cave,

00:05:14.340 --> 00:05:17.620
as Elsa learns the whole truth about Arendelle's
dark past.

00:05:17.960 --> 00:05:22.030
It's a great example of something that starts
off simple being expounded on in a really

00:05:22.030 --> 00:05:23.100
compelling way.

